# Vulnerabilities waived for the container image scan in .github/workflows/release.yml.
#
# The gate blocks a release on CRITICAL and HIGH findings **that have a fix available**; unfixed ones
# are already excluded by `--ignore-unfixed` and do not belong here. So an entry in this file means
# something narrower and more deliberate: *a fix exists and we are shipping without it anyway*.
#
# Every entry needs a reason and a date on the line above it, and the reason has to say why the fix
# cannot be taken rather than that it is inconvenient — a waiver with no expiry is a gate that has
# been quietly switched off. The usual right answer instead is to bump the base image digest in the
# Dockerfile, which is one line and takes effect on the next build.
#
# Format: one CVE id per line, `#` comments. Trivy reads this file from the repository root.
#
# Example of the shape an entry must take:
#
#   # 2026-08-12 — CVE-2026-00000 in the JRE base layer. Fixed in temurin 25.0.4, which regresses
#   # <thing>; revisit when 25.0.5 ships. Not reachable from our code path: <why>.
#   CVE-2026-00000
#
# 2026-08-14 — CVE-2026-39821 and CVE-2026-46600 in the Go standard library (stdlib v1.26.5) as
# compiled into /usr/bin/pebble, which ships inside eclipse-temurin:25-jre. Fixed in Go 1.26.6, and
# **the base-image bump was tried**: #483 raised the temurin digest to sha256:a214efa3… and the scan
# on that merge still reported Total: 2 (HIGH: 2). Temurin has not rebuilt against a patched Go, so
# the one-line fix this file's header recommends does not exist yet.
#
# Not reachable from our code path: pebble is Canonical's service manager, present in the base layer
# and never executed. Both backend Dockerfiles set ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "application.jar"] and
# run as USER 1000:1000; neither the Punycode nor the DNS-parsing path can be entered without pebble
# running and parsing hostile input, and it does not run.
#
# Revisit on every base-image bump, and delete both lines the moment Temurin ships a Go >= 1.26.6
# build — tracked in #492, which also holds the alternative of a base image without pebble.
CVE-2026-39821
CVE-2026-46600

# 2026-08-17 — SIX MORE OF EXACTLY THE SAME THING, and the count is the point (#492).
#
# Same binary, same frozen toolchain: /usr/bin/pebble in eclipse-temurin:25-jre is built from Go
# stdlib v1.26.5, so every Go stdlib CVE published from now on lands here. Three days after the two
# above were waived, six more had accumulated:
#
#   CVE-2026-33818  encoding/asn1    DoS — excessive recursion in Unmarshal
#   CVE-2026-56853  net/http         DoS — unencrypted HTTP/2 connections
#   CVE-2026-56858  html/template    XSS — pathological input
#   CVE-2026-56859  encoding/xml     DoS — decoding recursion depth
#   CVE-2026-56860  net/url          DoS — quadratic complexity in path handling
#   CVE-2026-56862  crypto/tls       DoS — indefinite KeyUpdate messages
#
# **The base-image bump was checked again first, as this file's header requires, and there is
# nothing to bump to.** `docker buildx imagetools inspect eclipse-temurin:25-jre` resolves to
# sha256:a214efa3… — byte for byte the digest both Dockerfiles already pin. Temurin still has not
# rebuilt against Go >= 1.26.6, so the one-line fix remains unavailable rather than merely untried.
#
# Verified locally rather than read off a CI log, against the pinned digest with the release
# workflow's own flags:
#
#   trivy image eclipse-temurin:25-jre@sha256:a214efa3… --severity CRITICAL,HIGH --ignore-unfixed
#
# All eight findings are in `usr/bin/pebble`, all `stdlib v1.26.5`. **Nothing outside pebble** — that
# is what makes one reachability argument cover the whole set rather than eight separate ones.
#
# Not reachable, for the same structural reason as above and not a per-CVE judgement: pebble is
# Canonical's service manager, it is present in the base layer, and it never runs. Both backend
# Dockerfiles set ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "application.jar"] and run as USER 1000:1000. Nothing
# starts pebble, so no ASN.1, XML, URL, TLS or template input ever reaches this code. An unreachable
# parser cannot be fed hostile input.
#
# **Do not extend this block a third time without doing #492's second option instead.** Waiving a
# growing list one batch at a time is how a gate dies quietly: the argument stays true while the
# habit rots, and the real fix — a base image with no pebble in it — keeps being deferred because
# each individual batch is cheap. Two entries was a waiver; eight is a base-image problem.
CVE-2026-33818
CVE-2026-56853
CVE-2026-56858
CVE-2026-56859
CVE-2026-56860
CVE-2026-56862
